content:
Goal: capture breadcrumbs and quick repros for the intermittent streaming failures seen during load tests (SSE / chunked responses).

Summary of issue:
- Under certain client patterns (slow consumer / flaky network) we see long pauses (~10-30s) in the SSE stream followed by a burst of many events OR truncated responses with socket errors (EPIPE / ECONNRESET).
- Problem appears more frequently when requests go through nginx/Envoy or through our dedicated proxy (Cloud Run tests).
- Symptoms: client sees no events for a while, then several come in quick succession, or connection drops with "socket hang up".

Observed logs / error snippets:
- 2025-01-23T14:12:05.432Z [streamer] wrote event batch size=1 res.write returned=false
- 2025-01-23T14:12:15.891Z [streamer] Error: write EPIPE at writeAfterFIN
- 2025-01-24T09:03:10.122Z [proxy-nginx] "client 10.0.0.5 timed out (110: Connection timed out) while reading response header from upstream"
- Client: "requests.exceptions.ChunkedEncodingError: ("

Hypotheses / root-cause candidates:
1) Backpressure not respected on server: we call res.write without checking return value. Node buffers until highWaterMark then returns false; need to wait for 'drain' event instead of slamming writes.
2) Proxy buffering: nginx/Envoy buffering (or compression) is aggregating small SSE chunks into larger ones or delaying flush, which makes slow clients experience bursts.
3) TCP-level delayed ACKs / Nagle + keepalive interactions: need socket.setNoDelay(true) on server side to avoid Nagle delays for tiny writes.
4) Misconfigured Content-Encoding (gzip) or transfer-encoding that triggers buffering in proxies.
5) A race where we call res.end while client still reading, producing EPIPE.

Quick repro (server) - minimal Node/Express SSE server:
1) server file: sse-server.js

const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.get('/sse', (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');
  res.flushHeaders && res.flushHeaders(); // if using compression middleware, ensure flushing
  let i = 0;
  const iv = setInterval(() => {
    const ok = res.write(`data: {\"i\": ${i}}\n\n`);
    console.log(new Date().toISOString(), 'wrote i=', i, 'ok=', ok);
    i++;
    if (i > 100) { clearInterval(iv); res.end(); }
    if (!ok) {
      console.log('buffer full, waiting for drain');
      res.once('drain', () => console.log('drain event'));
    }
  }, 50);
});
app.listen(3000, () => console.log('sse server listening 3000'));

Run: node sse-server.js

Repro client commands:
- Simple curl (no buffering): curl -v -N http://localhost:3000/sse
- Simulate slow consumer: curl --limit-rate 1024 -N http://localhost:3000/sse  # 1KB/s
- Python slow consumer:
import requests
import time
resp = requests.get('http://localhost:3000/sse', stream=True)
for chunk in resp.iter_content(chunk_size=16):
    time.sleep(0.2)
    print('got', chunk)

- Node client (fetch + streaming):
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
(async () => {
  const r = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/sse');
  const reader = r.body.getReader();
  while(true){ const {done,value}=await reader.read(); if(done) break; console.log('recv', value.length); await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,200)); }
})();

Proxy / nginx repro notes:
- Test with nginx in front (default config) often shows buffering and larger delays. For nginx make sure to disable buffering and HTTP/1.1 chunk buffering:
  proxy_buffering off;
  proxy_buffer_size 8k;
  proxy_http_version 1.1;
  proxy_set_header Connection '';
  gzip off;
  # If using X-Accel-Buffering: set X-Accel-Buffering: no on upstream code.

Observed that when proxy_buffering is left on we rarely see backpressure behavior on origin (res.write returns true) because nginx ACKs quickly after buffering; but client sees bursts. So behavior changes depending on proxy config.

Specific reproduction (with nginx):
- Run server on localhost:3000, nginx config reverse proxy to /sse, then run slow curl. Compare responses with proxy_buffering on vs off.

Instrumentation to add (server):
- Log res.write return value and timestamps (already in minimal repro).
- Log socket.bufferSize and res.socket.writableLength (Node >= 16 exposes writableLength) to see buffered bytes.
- On 'finish' and 'close' events log stack + last writes.
- Add per-connection counters for outstanding writes and last drain time.

Workarounds tried (and effects):
- Added socket.setNoDelay(true) immediately after handshake -> reduced some of the Nagle-induced batching; small improvement but not full fix.
- Switched off gzip/compression middleware -> improved immediate flush behavior.
- Ingested 'res.flush()' (from compression/express) -> helped when used with X-Accel-Buffering=no in nginx.
- Respect res.write() return value and wait for 'drain' -> prevents memory growth but increases perceived latency for events when consumer is slow (but correct).

Next steps / TODOs:
- [ ] Add quick monitoring metric: per-conn current writableLength and number of pending writes. Ship as lightweight probe to fetch during load tests. (INF-2345 ticket created)
- [ ] Run A/B load tests with nginx proxy_buffering on vs off and with gzip on vs off, capture latencies and drop rates.
- [ ] Update server runtime to always call socket.setNoDelay(true) for SSE endpoints.
- [ ] Add unit/integration test that simulates slow client (limit-rate) and asserts server respects backpressure (i.e., no unbounded memory growth).
- [ ] Add note to docs: "SSE endpoints must set X-Accel-Buffering: no and disable compression when behind nginx/Envoy".

Notes to self / random ideas:
- Consider switching to chunked JSON where each chunk is >1k to avoid tiny writes. But that trades off latency for throughput. Might be configurable per endpoint.
- Check Envoy docs for "streaming" and "enable_chunked_encoding" flags; there might be proxy-level settings that cause buffering.
- For Hosted API we should detect slow downstream (client) and opt to send fewer updates or switch to server-initiated backoff messages to reduce wasted compute.

References & links (copied here for convenience):
- INF-2345 (ticket): load tests show SSE stalls and EPIPEs - https://redwood.atlassian.net/browse/INF-2345
- PR that added SSE handler: https://github.com/redwood-inference/serving-runtime/pull/742
- Nginx reference: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_buffering

If I forget later: contact Samir (he has the perf test harness), Noah (ran the Cloud Run repro), and put quick notes in the issue.

-- Elena T.
